WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.279
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:08.830
One of the holy grail problems of machine
learning research is to achieve artificial

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.969
general intelligence, AGI in short.

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:17.449
Deep Blue was able to defeat the genius Kasparov
in Chess, but it was unable to tell us what

00:00:17.449 --> 00:00:19.000
the time was.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.150
Algorithms of this type we often refer to
as a weak AI, or narrow AI, a technique that

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:30.599
excels, or is maybe even on a superhuman level
at a task, but has zero or no knowledge about

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:31.599
anything else.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:36.420
A key to extend these algorithms would be
to design them in a way that their knowledge

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.130
generalizes well to other problems.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:43.579
This is what we call transfer learning, and
this collaboration between the Stanford AI

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:49.550
lab and Caltech goes by the name Neural Task
Programming, and tries to tackle this problem.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.649
A solution to practically any problem we're
trying to solve can be written as series of

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:55.649
tasks.

00:00:55.649 --> 00:01:00.579
These are typically complex actions, like
cleaning a table, or performing a backflip

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:03.300
that are difficult to transfer to a different
problem.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.640
This technique is a bit like divide and conquer
type algorithms that aggressively try to decompose

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.880
big, difficult tasks into smaller, more manageable
pieces.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.300
The smaller and easier to understand the pieces
are, the more reusable they are and the better

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:19.560
they generalize.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.020
Let's have a look at an example.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.970
For instance, in a problem where we need to
pick and place objects, this series of tasks

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.430
can be decomposed into picking and placing.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:35.540
These can be further diced into a series of
even smaller tasks, such as gripping, moving,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:36.540
and releasing actions.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:41.870
However, if the learning takes place like
this, we can now specify different variations

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:46.970
of these tasks, and the algorithm will quickly
understand how to adapt the structure of these

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:50.360
small tasks to efficiently to solve new problems.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:55.510
The new algorithm generalizes really well
for tasks with different lengths, topologies,

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:56.960
and changing objectives.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.540
If you take a look at the paper, you'll also
find some more information on adversarial

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:06.470
dynamics, which lists some problem variants
where a really unpleasant adversary pushes

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.670
things around on the table from time to time
to mess with the program, and there are some

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.630
results that show that the algorithm is able
to recover from these failure states quite

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:16.630
well.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:17.630
Really cool.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:22.120
Now, please don't take this as a complete
solution for AGI, because it is a fantastic

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.770
piece of work, but it's definitely not that.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:29.380
However, it may be a valuable puzzle piece
to build towards the final solution.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.630
This is research.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.780
We advance one step at a time.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.300
Man, what an amazing time to be alive.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

